1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:00,937
Match point.

2
00:00:00,937 --> 00:00:04,284
You've hit this serve a thousand
times in practice.

3
00:00:04,284 --> 00:00:07,567
Now your arm feels like it
belongs to someone else.

4
00:00:07,917 --> 00:00:12,205
Or it's a penalty to win the game, and
your legs forget how to kick.

5
00:00:12,555 --> 00:00:13,584
That's choking.

6
00:00:13,584 --> 00:00:17,248
It can happen to anyone, from club
players to superstars.

7
00:00:17,848 --> 00:00:19,338
Nerves won't disappear.

8
00:00:19,338 --> 00:00:22,255
But you can learn to play
your best with them.

9
00:00:22,255 --> 00:00:26,104
Today I'll show you four skills that
help you do exactly that.

10
00:00:26,454 --> 00:00:31,062
Choking means playing worse than you normally
would, right when it matters most.

11
00:00:31,412 --> 00:00:34,214
Scientists have two main explanations.

12
00:00:34,214 --> 00:00:36,154
The first is overthinking.

13
00:00:36,154 --> 00:00:41,759
Under pressure, you start controlling a move
that normally runs on autopilot,

14
00:00:41,759 --> 00:00:43,124
and it falls apart.

15
00:00:43,474 --> 00:00:46,070
It's like singing a song you
know by heart,

16
00:00:46,070 --> 00:00:48,547
but trying to remember every single note.

17
00:00:48,547 --> 00:00:49,255
You stumble.

18
00:00:49,605 --> 00:00:50,967
The second is worry.

19
00:00:50,967 --> 00:00:56,092
Worry eats up your attention and drags it
toward threats, like the goalkeeper,

20
00:00:56,092 --> 00:00:57,584
the crowd or the score.

21
00:00:57,934 --> 00:01:03,374
In one study, anxious penalty takers looked at
the goalkeeper sooner and for longer,

22
00:01:03,374 --> 00:01:04,846
and shot closer to him.

23
00:01:05,196 --> 00:01:06,924
And mistakes snowball.

24
00:01:06,924 --> 00:01:10,081
In over 650,000 Grand Slam tennis points,

25
00:01:10,081 --> 00:01:15,116
pressure and a previous error both made
the next error more likely.

26
00:01:15,466 --> 00:01:16,800
So here's the plan.

27
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:18,135
Train the pressure.

28
00:01:18,135 --> 00:01:19,469
Reframe the nerves.

29
00:01:19,469 --> 00:01:20,070
Lock in.

30
00:01:20,070 --> 00:01:21,738
And reset after mistakes.

31
00:01:22,088 --> 00:01:26,696
Skill one: practise with something at stake,
so the big day feels familiar.

32
00:01:27,046 --> 00:01:28,849
Think of it like a vaccine.

33
00:01:28,849 --> 00:01:31,681
A small, safe dose of
pressure in training,

34
00:01:31,681 --> 00:01:34,321
so the real thing doesn't knock you over.

35
00:01:34,671 --> 00:01:40,047
A review of 14 studies found pressure training
beat normal training when it came to

36
00:01:40,047 --> 00:01:41,711
performing under pressure.

37
00:01:42,061 --> 00:01:46,818
It worked for beginners and experienced players,
and for different kinds of skills.

38
00:01:47,168 --> 00:01:52,872
In a classic experiment, novice golfers who
practised putting while being filmed and

39
00:01:52,872 --> 00:01:54,684
judged didn't choke later.

40
00:01:54,684 --> 00:01:57,301
The golfers who practised normally did.

41
00:01:57,651 --> 00:01:59,318
Here's how to build it.

42
00:01:59,318 --> 00:02:01,333
Drill one: consequence sets.

43
00:02:01,333 --> 00:02:04,667
Take 10 penalties, free throws
or short serves.

44
00:02:04,667 --> 00:02:08,766
Every miss costs a small forfeit, or
goes on a leaderboard.

45
00:02:09,116 --> 00:02:13,063
Drill two: film yourself, and have a
teammate score you out of 10.

46
00:02:13,413 --> 00:02:15,630
Drill three: must-make ladders.

47
00:02:15,630 --> 00:02:17,916
Make 5 in a row, or start again.

48
00:02:17,916 --> 00:02:21,242
Or play first-to-3 shootouts
against a teammate.

49
00:02:21,592 --> 00:02:25,860
A good starting point is one or two
sets, once or twice a week,

50
00:02:25,860 --> 00:02:27,928
at the end of a normal session.

51
00:02:28,278 --> 00:02:30,761
Keep the forfeits light and friendly.

52
00:02:30,761 --> 00:02:35,595
Never humiliating, and never extra exercise
when you're already exhausted.

53
00:02:35,945 --> 00:02:37,314
The common mistake?

54
00:02:37,314 --> 00:02:42,447
Only practising in calm, easy drills, and
hoping more reps will be enough.

55
00:02:42,447 --> 00:02:47,444
In the studies, the same practice done
under pressure beat calm practice.

56
00:02:47,794 --> 00:02:51,348
Skill two: your heart is pounding
before the big moment.

57
00:02:51,348 --> 00:02:52,284
That's normal.

58
00:02:52,284 --> 00:02:54,279
What matters is how you read it.

59
00:02:54,629 --> 00:02:58,204
It's the same engine with a
different dashboard light.

60
00:02:58,204 --> 00:03:00,154
The revs are high either way.

61
00:03:00,154 --> 00:03:02,949
You decide whether it says
danger or ready.

62
00:03:03,299 --> 00:03:06,213
Scientists call this threat
versus challenge.

63
00:03:06,213 --> 00:03:08,051
Both come with high arousal.

64
00:03:08,051 --> 00:03:11,662
In a challenge state, you believe
you have what it takes.

65
00:03:12,012 --> 00:03:14,681
In a study of 60 experienced golfers,

66
00:03:14,681 --> 00:03:20,439
the ones guided into a challenge mindset putted
better, and did less overthinking.

67
00:03:20,789 --> 00:03:22,974
Most people try to calm down.

68
00:03:22,974 --> 00:03:27,125
In one survey, over 90% thought
that was the best tactic.

69
00:03:27,475 --> 00:03:33,077
But in one experiment, people who said "I'm
excited" out loud before singing karaoke

70
00:03:33,077 --> 00:03:36,834
sang more accurately than people
who said "I'm anxious".

71
00:03:36,834 --> 00:03:41,448
In a separate check, saying it didn't
slow their racing hearts at all.

72
00:03:41,798 --> 00:03:47,046
To be fair, those studies were about singing,
public speaking and maths, not sport.

73
00:03:47,396 --> 00:03:53,150
And in sport, the few studies of quick reframing
cues haven't shown a clear benefit

74
00:03:53,150 --> 00:03:53,492
yet.

75
00:03:53,492 --> 00:03:57,465
So treat it as a free tool worth
trying, not a proven fix.

76
00:03:57,815 --> 00:04:01,248
Drill one: before your key
moment, say it out loud.

77
00:04:01,248 --> 00:04:02,106
I'm excited.

78
00:04:02,106 --> 00:04:04,087
This is my body getting ready.

79
00:04:04,437 --> 00:04:09,549
Drill two: before a match, write down 3 things
you've done in training that prepare

80
00:04:09,549 --> 00:04:10,218
you for it.

81
00:04:10,569 --> 00:04:12,214
What about deep breathing?

82
00:04:12,214 --> 00:04:15,200
Slow breathing does shift your
body toward rest.

83
00:04:15,200 --> 00:04:18,185
But for sport performance, the
results are mixed.

84
00:04:18,535 --> 00:04:22,716
So use one slow breath as part of
your routine, not as a cure.

85
00:04:23,066 --> 00:04:28,426
And if your nerves are constant, cause panic
attacks, or stop you sleeping,

86
00:04:28,426 --> 00:04:33,221
eating or wanting to play, talk to a
sport psychologist or a doctor.

87
00:04:33,571 --> 00:04:36,707
If you're under 18, tell a
coach or parent too.

88
00:04:37,057 --> 00:04:39,916
Skill three: one routine,
one look, one cue.

89
00:04:40,265 --> 00:04:44,647
A routine is the same short set of
actions before every key shot,

90
00:04:44,647 --> 00:04:48,564
like a pilot's checklist that ends
with eyes on the runway.

91
00:04:48,914 --> 00:04:52,126
A big review found routines
improved performance,

92
00:04:52,126 --> 00:04:54,631
and the benefit held up under pressure.

93
00:04:54,981 --> 00:04:55,927
Then the look.

94
00:04:55,927 --> 00:05:00,720
The quiet eye is your final, steady look at
the target just before you move.

95
00:05:01,070 --> 00:05:03,654
Experts hold it longer than beginners.

96
00:05:03,654 --> 00:05:08,622
And across training studies, practising the
quiet eye improved performance.

97
00:05:08,972 --> 00:05:12,693
In one football study, players who
called out their corner,

98
00:05:12,693 --> 00:05:15,298
held their eyes on it for about 1 second,

99
00:05:15,298 --> 00:05:19,639
and then kicked were the only group to
improve in a pressure shootout.

100
00:05:19,989 --> 00:05:21,020
And don't rush.

101
00:05:21,020 --> 00:05:26,433
In major tournament shootouts, players who
started their run-up within about half a

102
00:05:26,433 --> 00:05:29,333
second of the whistle scored
68% of the time.

103
00:05:29,683 --> 00:05:33,192
Players who waited a moment
longer scored 82%.

104
00:05:33,192 --> 00:05:37,747
Rushing might be a sign of nerves,
but either way, slow down.

105
00:05:38,097 --> 00:05:41,789
Finally, one cue word, about the
target, not your body.

106
00:05:41,789 --> 00:05:44,558
Something like corner, through or smooth.

107
00:05:44,558 --> 00:05:48,316
Self-talk like this helps,
especially for precise skills.

108
00:05:48,665 --> 00:05:51,262
Drill one: build a 4-step routine.

109
00:05:51,262 --> 00:05:54,453
Set up, breathe, eyes on target, cue word.

110
00:05:54,453 --> 00:05:55,121
Then go.

111
00:05:55,121 --> 00:05:59,054
Use it on every rep in training,
not just in matches.

112
00:05:59,755 --> 00:06:01,920
Drill two: the quiet-eye drill.

113
00:06:01,920 --> 00:06:06,454
Call your target, hold your eyes on it
for about 1 second, then go.

114
00:06:06,803 --> 00:06:11,590
Start with at least 10 reps a session, and
keep it up for at least 3 weeks.

115
00:06:11,590 --> 00:06:14,739
That's roughly what one small
football study used.

116
00:06:15,089 --> 00:06:18,921
The common mistake is changing your
routine in the big moment,

117
00:06:18,921 --> 00:06:21,233
or speeding it up to get it over with.

118
00:06:21,583 --> 00:06:27,187
Skill four: one mistake raises the chance of
another, especially on the big points.

119
00:06:27,187 --> 00:06:28,388
That's the spiral.

120
00:06:28,739 --> 00:06:32,005
A reset is like closing all
your browser tabs.

121
00:06:32,005 --> 00:06:36,035
Close the last point, and open one
fresh tab for the next.

122
00:06:36,385 --> 00:06:39,434
This part has less direct
research behind it.

123
00:06:39,434 --> 00:06:44,406
It takes what works for routines and
self-talk, and uses it between points.

124
00:06:44,756 --> 00:06:46,707
The reset has three steps.

125
00:06:46,707 --> 00:06:49,597
Release: turn away, or wipe your hands.

126
00:06:49,597 --> 00:06:51,765
Breathe: one slow breath out.

127
00:06:51,765 --> 00:06:54,655
Refocus: one cue word for the next play.

128
00:06:55,005 --> 00:06:57,437
Then go straight into your normal routine.

129
00:06:57,787 --> 00:07:02,537
Drill one: in training games, run the
reset after every single error,

130
00:07:02,537 --> 00:07:04,166
so it becomes automatic.

131
00:07:04,515 --> 00:07:06,611
Drill two: write a what-if plan.

132
00:07:06,611 --> 00:07:10,232
If I miss, I'll jog back, clap my
teammate, and say next.

133
00:07:10,583 --> 00:07:16,514
Drill three: start practice games from a bad
score, like 0-30 or two goals down.

134
00:07:16,863 --> 00:07:19,461
Keep your self-talk kind and useful.

135
00:07:19,461 --> 00:07:21,919
Beating yourself up is not a reset.

136
00:07:22,269 --> 00:07:27,300
The common mistake is replaying the error,
or chasing it with a risky shot.

137
00:07:27,300 --> 00:07:30,610
Errors cluster under pressure,
so break the chain.

138
00:07:30,961 --> 00:07:32,429
Here's a simple week.

139
00:07:32,429 --> 00:07:37,501
Early in the week, write your routine, your
cue word and your what-if plan.

140
00:07:37,501 --> 00:07:40,838
Practise the routine with quiet
eye, 3 sets of 10.

141
00:07:41,188 --> 00:07:43,034
Midweek, a pressure block.

142
00:07:43,034 --> 00:07:45,495
Ten reps with consequences, filmed,

143
00:07:45,495 --> 00:07:49,529
with the routine on every rep and a
reset after every miss.

144
00:07:49,879 --> 00:07:54,402
Later, play games from bad scores, and
finish with a must-make ladder.

145
00:07:54,752 --> 00:07:58,750
On match day, use the same routine
on every key moment.

146
00:07:58,750 --> 00:08:01,749
Don't rush, and reset after every mistake.

147
00:08:02,099 --> 00:08:05,953
Before the match, train the pressure
and reframe the nerves.

148
00:08:05,953 --> 00:08:07,217
During it, lock in.

149
00:08:07,217 --> 00:08:08,670
After a mistake, reset.

150
00:08:09,020 --> 00:08:11,784
The nerves will still show up
at match point.

151
00:08:11,784 --> 00:08:14,908
This time, you'll know exactly
what to do with them.
